**Read:** [Guideline for Safe Practices for Cryogenic Air Separation Plants — EIGA 147/22](../sources/Cryogenic-Air-Separation-EIGA.lingua#r_39BST0TM9XV9VEZ14NXVJW7TWA), air pretreatment; [On-site nitrogen generators for food use — EIGA 194/24](../sources/Onsite-Nitrogen-Generators-EIGA.lingua#r_2VHG044YCF1X5GZRJVJJFN7NFH), feed conditioning; [Gases and Applications — Linde](../sources/Gases-and-Applications-Linde.lingua#r_72580QNZ60FMQAARB8ZG7SRTFT), water impurity and specification conventions; [Outgassing properties of vacuum materials — Paolo Chiggiato](../sources/Outgassing-Properties-Chiggiato.lingua#r_18MM2A316NWRJABS8E53468PWT), water on surfaces.

Cooling can remove water by condensation; a coalescing filter removes entrained droplets; an adsorbent can remove vapor. These are different mechanisms. A particle or aerosol filter does not establish a low molecular water concentration.

Dew point depends on water partial pressure and the stated total-pressure basis. Pressure dew point and an expanded sample's dew point need conversion through the amount fraction and equilibrium relation. In a very clean system, water released from walls and sample lines can dominate a transient even when incoming gas is dry.

**Work:** Trace water in vapor, droplets, ice and surface reservoirs through a supply path. Convert between a declared water amount fraction and dew point using an identified vapor-pressure relation within its range. Explain which changes follow from pressure reduction alone and which imply additional adsorption, condensation or leakage.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
